Here's a Python script that uses BeautifulSoup to extract the URLs from the given website:

```python
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

def get_url(base_url, start_index):
    return f"{base_url}?startIndex={start_index}&pageSize=20&areaFlg=I&orderBy=issueDate"

def extract_urls(url):
    response = requests.get(url)
    soup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, 'html.parser')

    urls = []
    for link in soup.find_all('a'):
        if link.get('href').startswith('/new/album/detail.htm'):
            urls.append(link.get('href'))

    return urls

def main():
    base_url = "https://www.melon.com/new/album/listPaging.htm?startIndex=1&pageSize=20&areaFlg=I&orderBy=issueDate"
    start_indexes = range(1, 182, 20)

    for start_index in start_indices:
        url = get_url(base_url, start_index)
        urls = extract_urls(url)

        for url in urls:
            print(url)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

This script will extract the URLs from the given website and print them to the console. The `get_url` function generates the URL with the specified `start_index`, and the `extract_urls` function uses BeautifulSoup to extract the URLs from the HTML content. The `main` function iterates over the `start_indexes` range and calls the `extract_urls` function for each index.

To create an Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns, follow these steps:

1. Open Google Sheets.
2. Click "Extensions" > "Apps Script." This will open the Google Apps Script editor.
3. In the editor, replace the existing code with the following:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var values = dataRange.getValues();

  var placeName = "New York"; // Replace this with the place name you want to search for
  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();

  for (var i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    var address = values[i][0];

    if (address.toLowerCase().includes(placeName.toLowerCase())) {
      geocoder.geocode({ 'address': address }, function(results, status) {
        if (status == 'OK') {
          var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
          var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

          sheet.getRange("B" + (i + 2)).setValue(latitude);
          sheet.getRange("C" + (i + 2)).setValue(longitude);
        } else {
          console.log('Geocode was not successful for the following reason: ' + status);
        }
      });
    }
  }
}
```

4. Save the script by clicking "File" > "Save."
5. To run the script, click "Run" > "Run function" and select "searchPlace" from the dropdown menu.

This script will search for the specified place name (in this case, "New York") in the first column of your Google Sheet. If it finds a match, it will store the latitude and longitude of that location in the second and third columns, respectively.

