Yes, you can divide an hour into 10 equal parts to perform more granular analysis by using the following formula in Excel:

`=TIME(HOUR(A1), MINUTE(A1)/6, SECOND(A1))`

This formula takes the time value in cell A1 (in the format "HH:MM:SS") and divides the minute value by 6 to get the value for every 6 minutes. The result is a new time value representing one of the 10 equal parts of the hour. You can then use this formula to create a new column with the divided values and perform your analysis on these values.

In MATLAB, you can achieve the same result using the following code:

```matlab
% Input data
data = [14, 30, 45, 0, 15, 30, 45, 0, 15, 30, 45, 0]; % Hourly measurements

% Divide each hour into 10 equal parts
num_parts = 10;
part_duration = 6; % Minutes per part

% Calculate the start time of each part
start_times = zeros(size(data, 1), num_parts);
for i = 1:size(data, 1)
    start_times(i, :) = data(i) - mod(data(i), part_duration) / 2;
end

% Create a new matrix with the divided values
divided_data = zeros(size(data, 1), num_parts);
for i = 1:size(data, 1)
    for j = 1:num_parts
        divided_data(i, j) = data(i) - start_times(i, j);
    end
end

% Plot the divided data
plot(divided_data);
xlabel('Time (Minutes)');
ylabel('Value');
title('Divided Hourly Measurements');
```

This code will divide each hour into 10 equal parts of 6 minutes each and create a new matrix with the divided values. You can then plot the divided data to visualize the results.

