To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package using NuGet.
2. Create a method to extract the username from the JWT token. Here's an example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;

public static string ExtractUsernameFromJwt(string jwtToken)
{
    var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
    var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        IssuerSigningKey = key,
        ValidateIssuer = false,
        ValidateAudience = false,
        ValidateLifetime = true,
        ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
    });

    var usernameClaim = token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/username");
    return usernameClaim?.Value;
}
```
Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your application's secret key.
3. Now, you can use this method in your controllers or other parts of your application to extract the username from the JWT token. For example:
```csharp
[HttpGet]
public string GetUserName()
{
    var jwtToken = Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Substring(7); // Assuming the JWT token is sent in the authorization header
    var username = ExtractUsernameFromJwt(jwtToken);
    return username;
}
```
This method will extract the username from the JWT token and return it as a string. You can then use this username to personalize the user experience or perform other actions.

