To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow for read-host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_input.ps1`) with the following content:
```powershell
# Read user input
$input = Read-Host "Enter some input:"

# Display the input
Write-Host "You entered: $input"
```
1. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), define a `local-exec` provisioner to execute the PowerShell script:
```hcl
provider "null" {
  version = "3.0.0"
}

resource "null_resource" "read_input" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_input.ps1"
  }
}
```
This will execute the `read_input.ps1` script when the resource is created or updated. The `local-exec` provisioner will run the command in the local environment, so you don't need to worry about setting up any remote connections or authentication.

When you apply the Terraform configuration, the PowerShell script will be executed and prompt the user for input. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform output.

Note that this example assumes that you have already installed PowerShell on your local machine. If you're using a different operating system or don't have PowerShell installed, you may need to adjust the command accordingly.

