To achieve this task, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install the Accord.NET NuGet package.
2. Load your dataset of labeled sentences.
3. Preprocess the data by converting it to a matrix of word counts using the CountVectorizer class.
4. Train the Naive Bayes classifier using the preprocessed data.
5. Predict the label for a new sentence using the trained classifier.

Here's a concise and elegant C# code that accomplishes this task:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Bayes;
using Accord.MachineLearning.FeatureExtractors;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Load your dataset of labeled sentences
        var dataset = new List<(string sentence, string label)>
        {
            ("Sports game was exciting", "Sports"),
            ("Politician made a controversial statement", "Politics"),
            ("New movie is a must-see", "Entertainment"),
            // Add more labeled sentences here
        };

        // Preprocess the data by converting it to a matrix of word counts using the CountVectorizer class
        var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer();
        var X = vectorizer.Transform(dataset.Select(x => x.sentence));

        // Train the Naive Bayes classifier using the preprocessed data
        var classifier = new NaiveBayesClassifier(X);
        classifier.Train();

        // Predict the label for a new sentence using the trained classifier
        var newSentence = "The sports game was exciting";
        var prediction = classifier.Predict(newSentence);

        Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {prediction}");
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace the `dataset` variable with your actual dataset of labeled sentences. Also, adjust the `newSentence` variable with the sentence you want to classify.

